Q: How to correctly apply the exponent to all fields in a Pyth Network Hermes API price response in TypeScript? A: <p>Yes, your approach is correct - the exponent must be applied to <strong>every</strong> numeric field in both the <code>price</code> and <code>ema_price</code> objects, not just <code>price.price</code>.</p>
<h3>Why the exponent applies to all fields</h3>
<p>Pyth publishers submit raw integer values for both price and confidence. The <code>expo</code> field is a shared scaling factor for the entire price component. From the <a href="https://docs.pyth.network/price-feeds/best-practices" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Pyth documentation</a>:</p>
<pre><code>real_value = raw_value * 10^expo
</code></pre>
<p>This applies equally to <code>price</code>, <code>conf</code>, <code>ema_price.price</code>, and <code>ema_price.conf</code>.</p>
<h3>Your parsing function with edge case handling</h3>
<p>Your <code>parsePythFields</code> function is correct. Here it is with defensive checks for the edge cases you asked about:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function parsePythFields(feed: PythPriceFeed) {
  const multiplier = Math.pow(10, feed.price.expo);

  const price    = Number(feed.price.price) * multiplier;
  const conf     = Number(feed.price.conf) * multiplier;
  const emaPrice = Number(feed.ema_price.price) * multiplier;
  const emaConf  = Number(feed.ema_price.conf) * multiplier;

  // Guard against zero/negative price before doing math with it
  if (price &lt;= 0) {
    throw new Error(`Invalid price: ${price} (raw: ${feed.price.price}, expo: ${feed.price.expo})`);
  }

  return { price, conf, emaPrice, emaConf };
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Edge cases to watch for</h3>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Zero prices</strong> — can happen briefly during feed initialization or if a feed goes stale. Always check before dividing (e.g. <code>conf / price * 100</code> would give <code>Infinity</code>).</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Positive exponents</strong> — rare, but possible for very small-value tokens. <code>Math.pow(10, expo)</code> handles both positive and negative exponents correctly, so no code change needed.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Stale data</strong> — check <code>publish_time</code> against the current time. If the price is older than your tolerance (e.g. 60 seconds), the feed may be inactive:</p>
</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const ageSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) - feed.price.publish_time;
if (ageSeconds &gt; 60) {
  console.warn(`Price is ${ageSeconds}s old — feed may be stale`);
}
</code></pre>
<h3>Common mistake</h3>
<p>The most common mistake (which you hit) is assuming <code>conf</code> is already in human-readable units. The raw <code>conf</code> value <code>&quot;119174553&quot;</code> with <code>expo: -8</code> is actually <code>1.19</code> USD — not 119 million.</p>
<p>I built <a href="https://market-witness.vercel.app" rel="nofollow noreferrer">The Market Witness</a> (<a href="https://github.com/Joestarsan/market-witness" rel="nofollow noreferrer">source</a>) using this exact parsing approach, which uses Pyth confidence intervals to evaluate trade quality.</p>

Q: &#39;const&#39; initializer in an ambient context must be a string or numeric literal A: <p>That's a nasty little detail: you were using a <code>=</code> where it has to be a <code>:</code></p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>export declare namespace MyNamespace {
    const myFunc: (a:number) =&gt; 3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>=</code> would be used with <code>type</code> as in</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>export declare namespace MyNamespace {
    type myFunc = (a:number) =&gt; 3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The difference becomes clear if you state it as</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>type myFunc = (a:number) =&gt; 3;
export declare namespace MyNamespace {
    const myFunc: myFunc;
}
</code></pre>
<p>i.e. <code>=</code> means defining a type and <code>:</code> applying a type (the &quot;normal&quot; TS <code>:</code>), so it is clear that TS will complain if you mix both.</p>
<p>Life would be much easier if TS would simply say: &quot;Could there be a typo here?&quot;</p>
<p>Curiously I have projects where TS does not complain about this error (same TS version and no other configs than in projects where this error is thrown).</p>

Q: Localization of numbers and dates is not working in Nuxt3 + VueI18n A: <p>Thanks a lot to JUBEI for his comment.</p>
<pre><code>// package.json
{  
    &quot;dependencies&quot;: {
        &quot;@nuxtjs/i18n&quot;: &quot;^10.2.0&quot;,
        &quot;nuxt&quot;: &quot;4.1.3&quot;,
        &quot;vue&quot;: &quot;^3.5.24&quot;,
    }
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// nuxt.config.ts
export default defineNuxtConfig({
    i18n: {
        langDir: &quot;locales&quot;, // Or other
        locales: [
            { code: &quot;en&quot;, language: &quot;en-US&quot;, file: &quot;en.json&quot; },
            { code: &quot;fr&quot;, language: &quot;fr-FR&quot;, file: &quot;fr.json&quot; },
        ],
        defaultLocale: &quot;en&quot;,
        strategy: &quot;no_prefix&quot;, // Or other 
    },
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// i18n/i18n.config.ts
import { datetimeFormats } from &quot;./datetime-formats&quot;;

export default defineI18nConfig(() =&gt; ({
    fallbackLocale: &quot;en&quot;,
    datetimeFormats,
}));
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// i18n/datetime-formats.ts
export const datetimeFormats = {
    fr: {
        short: {
            day: &quot;2-digit&quot;,
            month: &quot;short&quot;,
            year: &quot;numeric&quot;,
        },
    },
    en: {
        short: {
            day: &quot;2-digit&quot;,
            month: &quot;short&quot;,
            year: &quot;numeric&quot;,
        },
    },
} as const;
</code></pre>
